import{_ as s,C as n,o as t,c as i,a3 as a,b as d,w as r,a as c,E as h,a4 as l}from"./chunks/framework.B02wg2PO.js";const w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Worktrees, Branches & Publishing","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"concepts/worktrees-and-branches.md","filePath":"concepts/worktrees-and-branches.md"}'),p={name:"concepts/worktrees-and-branches.md"};function u(g,e,b,k,m,f){const o=n("Mermaid");return t(),i("div",null,[e[1]||(e[1]=a('<h1 id="worktrees-branches-publishing" tabindex="-1">Worktrees, Branches &amp; Publishing <a class="header-anchor" href="#worktrees-branches-publishing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Worktrees, Branches &amp; Publishing&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A run that touches code accumulates its work on <strong>one branch per project</strong>, in <strong>one private git worktree per project</strong>, and commits <strong>locally</strong>. Nothing reaches origin until you explicitly publish.</p><h2 id="one-branch-per-run-per-project" tabindex="-1">One branch per run, per project <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-branch-per-run-per-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One branch per run, per project&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The branch name is derived from the run&#39;s title and the tail of its id:</p><div class="language-ts vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">ts</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">`saidi/run-${</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">slugify</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">title</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">)</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">}-${</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">runId</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">split</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&#39;-&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">).</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">pop</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">()</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">}`</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>slugify</code> lowercases, collapses everything non-alphanumeric to <code>-</code>, trims leading and trailing dashes, and truncates to 40 characters (falling back to <code>change</code> if nothing survives). So a run titled &quot;Fix the checkout total&quot; produces something like <code>saidi/run-fix-the-checkout-total-4f2a</code>.</p><p>The name is <strong>stable and deterministic</strong>, and that is the point: it is named after the <strong>run</strong>, not after each task, so every delegation in the run accumulates on the same branch instead of cutting a fresh one each time. Every project delegation passes it as <code>newBranchName</code> alongside <code>reuseBranch: participant.branch</code>, and if the deterministic name already exists (a first delegation after a crash or repair) it is reused rather than forked.</p><p>The branch a participant is on is tracked on the run&#39;s <code>RunParticipant.branch</code> record and captured the moment it is decided — before editing starts — so a process that dies mid-edit can resume the same worker session on the same branch. A branch that has been deleted out of band is <strong>cleared</strong> rather than handed forward as reusable.</p><p>Runs also pass <code>keepEmptyBranch: true</code>: a delegation that changed nothing keeps the run branch, because the branch <em>is</em> the run&#39;s shared context. Other callers tidy up an empty fresh cut.</p><h2 id="each-project-gets-an-isolated-worktree" tabindex="-1">Each project gets an isolated worktree <a class="header-anchor" href="#each-project-gets-an-isolated-worktree" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Each project gets an isolated worktree&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A repo has one working tree, so every path that touches it has to serialise on the repo lock. The old model checked the run branch out, edited, committed, and checked the base back out — which meant two runs on one project proceeded strictly turn by turn, and the checkout dance itself produced edits that vanished when another delegation restored the base.</p><p>Instead, each run gets its own git worktree per project: its own HEAD, its own index, its own files, sharing the repo&#39;s object store.</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>memory/runs/&lt;runId&gt;/repos/&lt;project&gt;/</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This is a guarantee rather than a convention, because git refuses to check one branch out in two worktrees:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>fatal: &#39;saidi/run-x&#39; is already checked out at …</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Cross-branch contamination becomes something the filesystem will not allow — and unlike an in-memory lock, that holds across processes. Two runs can edit the same file in the same repo at the same time, each committing only its own change, while the shared tree stays on its original branch and clean.</p><h3 id="how-the-base-ref-is-chosen" tabindex="-1">How the base ref is chosen <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-base-ref-is-chosen" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the base ref is chosen&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The base is <strong>resolved explicitly and passed to <code>git worktree add</code></strong>, never inherited from whatever HEAD happens to be. <code>resolveBaseRef</code> tries, in order, the caller&#39;s preferred ref (the project&#39;s policy branch) then <code>staging</code>, <code>develop</code>, <code>main</code>, <code>master</code> — and for each name it prefers the remote-tracking ref <code>origin/&lt;name&gt;</code> over the local branch, because a stale local branch is worse than no local branch. If none exist it falls back to <code>origin/HEAD</code>.</p><p>Reading <code>git symbolic-ref --short HEAD</code> instead is how a repo under a staging-only policy produced a branch cut from <code>main</code>.</p><h3 id="creation-reuse-and-hydration" tabindex="-1">Creation, reuse, and hydration <a class="header-anchor" href="#creation-reuse-and-hydration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Creation, reuse, and hydration&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>ensureRunWorktree</code> has a lock-free fast path: if the directory already exists and its HEAD is the run branch, it is returned with <code>created: false</code>. Otherwise it takes the repo lock and:</p><ol><li>Re-checks under the lock (a concurrent delegation for the same run may have won).</li><li>Removes and re-creates any <strong>stale</strong> directory left by a crashed run — a half-created tree is never adopted — then runs <code>git worktree prune</code>.</li><li>Resolves the base and runs <code>git fetch origin --prune</code>.</li><li><code>git worktree add &lt;dir&gt; &lt;branch&gt;</code> when the branch already exists, otherwise <code>git worktree add -b &lt;branch&gt; &lt;dir&gt; &lt;base&gt;</code>.</li><li>Hydrates the tree with what git does not carry.</li></ol><p>Hydration matters because a fresh worktree has no dependencies and no local config:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>What</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>node_modules</code></td><td>A copy-on-write clone first (APFS <code>cp -c</code>, or <code>cp --reflink=auto</code>), falling back to a symlink. Never <code>npm ci</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>.env</code>, <code>.env.local</code>, <code>.env.development</code>, <code>.env.development.local</code>, <code>.env.staging</code></td><td>Copied into every package root (repo root plus one or two levels, e.g. <code>apps/*</code>, <code>packages/*</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A CoW clone gives each run a genuinely private tree, so two concurrent builds cannot fight over <code>node_modules/.cache</code>. A partial copy is deleted rather than kept — it type-checks as &quot;installed&quot; and then fails deep.</p><p>Uncommitted work <strong>survives between delegations</strong>: a second call for the same run returns the same path with <code>created: false</code> and the tree untouched.</p><h3 id="when-there-is-no-worktree" tabindex="-1">When there is no worktree <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-there-is-no-worktree" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When there is no worktree&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>runWorktreeFor</code> returns <code>null</code> — and the caller falls back to the shared tree under the repo lock, exactly as before — in three cases:</p><ul><li>the <code>runWorktrees</code> setting is off (it defaults on);</li><li>the project&#39;s policy is <code>branch: current</code>, which commits <em>onto</em> the base branch (git will not check one branch out twice, so <code>current</code> and worktrees are mutually exclusive by design);</li><li><code>git worktree add</code> failed, most often because the branch is already checked out somewhere else. The half-created directory is removed and the run continues on the shared tree.</li></ul><p>Worktree setup never fails a delegation outright: any thrown error also resolves to <code>null</code>.</p><h2 id="run-work-commits-locally" tabindex="-1">Run work commits locally <a class="header-anchor" href="#run-work-commits-locally" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Run work commits locally&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every run delegation calls <code>runProjectWork</code> with <code>push: false</code>. After the agent&#39;s edits:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> add</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -A</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> commit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -m</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;&lt;first line of the task, ≤100 chars&gt;</span></span>\n<span class="line"></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">(via Saidi)&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>and the change note reads:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Committed on `saidi/run-…` (local).</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The commit subject is deliberately the first line of the task, bounded — a full delegation prompt as a commit message is how planning prose ended up in a repo&#39;s history. The full task stays in the run thread.</p><p>If the agent committed the work itself, the tree is clean but HEAD moved; that is detected and treated as a real change rather than reported as &quot;no changes were needed&quot;.</p><p>When the project policy declares a <code>verify:</code> command it runs here, on the branch, and its pass/fail is reported mechanically in the change note. See <a href="/docs/reference/project-policy">Project Policy</a>.</p><h2 id="worktree-publish-lifecycle" tabindex="-1">Worktree + publish lifecycle <a class="header-anchor" href="#worktree-publish-lifecycle" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Worktree + publish lifecycle&quot;">​</a></h2>',39)),(t(),d(l,null,{default:r(()=>[h(o,{id:"mermaid-172",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20TD%0A%20%20%20%20S(%5B%22Project%20delegation%20for%20a%20run%22%5D)%20--%3E%20E%5B%22ensureRunWorktree%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20E%20--%3E%20C%7B%22dir%20exists%2C%5CnHEAD%20%3D%20run%20branch%3F%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20C%20--%3E%7Cyes%2C%20fast%20path%7C%20RE%5B%22Reused%2C%20created%3A%20false%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20C%20--%3E%7Cno%7C%20LK%5B%22Take%20repo%20lock%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20LK%20--%3E%20STALE%7B%22stale%20dir%5Cnfrom%20a%20crash%3F%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20STALE%20--%3E%7Cyes%7C%20RM%5B%22Remove%20%2B%20git%20worktree%20prune%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20STALE%20--%3E%7Cno%7C%20BASE%0A%20%20%20%20RM%20--%3E%20BASE%5B%22Resolve%20base%20ref%5Cn(policy%20branch%20%E2%86%92%20staging%20%E2%86%92%20develop%5Cn%E2%86%92%20main%20%E2%86%92%20master%20%E2%86%92%20origin%2FHEAD)%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20BASE%20--%3E%20FETCH%5B%22git%20fetch%20origin%20--prune%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20FETCH%20--%3E%20ADD%5B%22git%20worktree%20add%5Cn(new%20-b%20branch%2C%20or%20reuse%20existing)%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20ADD%20--%3E%20HYD%5B%22Hydrate%3A%20node_modules%20(CoW%20clone)%2C%5Cn.env*%20files%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20HYD%20--%3E%20WORK%5B%22Agent%20edits%20in%5Cnmemory%2Fruns%2F%3CrunId%3E%2Frepos%2F%3Cproject%3E%2F%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20RE%20--%3E%20WORK%0A%20%20%20%20WORK%20--%3E%20COMMIT%5B%22git%20add%20-A%20%26%26%20git%20commit%5Cn(push%3A%20false%20%E2%80%94%20local%20only)%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20COMMIT%20--%3E%7Cmore%20delegations%20on%20this%20run%7C%20WORK%0A%20%20%20%20COMMIT%20--%3E%7Cpublish%20tool%20%2F%20PUBLISH%20button%7C%20PUB%5B%22publishRunBranch%5Cn(under%20repo%20lock)%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20PUB%20--%3E%20PB%5B%22Resolve%20PR%20base%20%3D%20origin%2FHEAD%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20PB%20--%3E%20PF%5B%22git%20fetch%20%2B%20--ff-only%20pull%20of%20base%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20PF%20--%3E%20CK%7B%22rev-list%20--count%5Cnbase..branch%20%3D%200%3F%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20CK%20--%3E%7Cyes%7C%20REF(%5B%22Refused%3A%20nothing%20to%20publish%22%5D)%0A%20%20%20%20CK%20--%3E%7Cno%7C%20PS%5B%22git%20push%20-u%20origin%20%3Cbranch%3E%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20PS%20--%3E%20PR(%5B%22PR%20opened%20against%20base%22%5D)%0A%20%20%20%20WORK%20-.-%3E%7Crun%20ends%20done%2Fcancelled%7C%20CLEAN%5B%22removeRunWorktrees%5Cn(git%20worktree%20remove%20--force%20%2B%20prune)%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20WORK%20-.-%3E%7Crun%20ends%20error%7C%20KEEP%5B%22Worktree%20kept%20for%20inspection%22%5D%0A"})]),fallback:r(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[c(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[2]||(e[2]=a('<h2 id="publishing-is-a-separate-explicit-step" tabindex="-1">Publishing is a separate, explicit step <a class="header-anchor" href="#publishing-is-a-separate-explicit-step" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Publishing is a separate, explicit step&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Pushing and opening a PR is not per-turn. It happens when you (or PRIME, via the <code>publish</code> run tool) ask for it — the same <code>publishRunProject</code> path backs both the tool and the CODE view&#39;s PUBLISH button, so both behave identically.</p><p>The branch to ship is chosen in this order: an explicit operator pick (CODE view), then the tracked participant branch, then the run&#39;s headline branch. With none of those you get a refusal telling you to make a change with <code>delegate</code> (mode <code>work</code>) first.</p><p><code>publishRunBranch</code> then, under the repo lock:</p><ol><li>Resolves the PR base from the repo&#39;s <strong>real</strong> default branch — <code>origin/HEAD</code> — falling back to the scanned project branch, then <code>main</code>. The scanned value can be stale.</li><li>Verifies <code>refs/heads/&lt;branch&gt;</code> still exists. A stale tracked branch fails fast with a way forward rather than pushing blind.</li><li>Pulls the latest base (<code>--ff-only</code> when it is checked out, otherwise a <code>fetch origin base:base</code>) so the PR reflects current remote state.</li><li>Refuses when <code>rev-list --count base..branch</code> is <code>0</code>: <em>&quot;has no commits beyond <code>&lt;base&gt;</code> — nothing to publish yet.&quot;</em></li><li><code>git push -u origin &lt;branch&gt;</code>.</li><li>Opens a pull request against the base.</li></ol><p>Outcomes:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Message</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pushed + PR opened</td><td><code>Pushed `branch` and opened a PR: &lt;url&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td>Pushed, PR not opened</td><td><code>Pushed `branch`; open a PR manually.</code></td></tr><tr><td>Push failed</td><td><code>Push failed for `branch`: &lt;last line of git output&gt;</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Either way the result is appended to the run thread as an event from the project.</p><h2 id="the-run-branch-and-the-project-s-default-branch" tabindex="-1">The run branch and the project&#39;s default branch <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-run-branch-and-the-project-s-default-branch" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The run branch and the project&#39;s default branch&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The default policy is <code>branch: new</code> — a <code>saidi/*</code> branch cut from the base, with the PR opened against the project&#39;s default branch. <code>branch: current</code> commits straight onto the checked-out branch instead, and is <strong>guarded</strong>: if that branch is <code>main</code>, <code>master</code>, or the project&#39;s declared branch, the work is refused unless the operator explicitly approved it.</p><p>The CODE view reads git state per project participant: <code>runBranch</code>, <code>baseBranch</code> (the project profile&#39;s branch, or <code>main</code>), the actually checked-out <code>currentBranch</code>, whether the tree is <code>dirty</code>, the <code>base..run</code> commits, the <code>base...run</code> changed files, and the branch&#39;s PR looked up live with <code>gh</code> (capped at five seconds). When run and base are the same branch — a <code>branch: current</code> project — it says so explicitly and shows the branch&#39;s recent commits instead of a range.</p><h2 id="dirty-trees-and-conflicting-branches" tabindex="-1">Dirty trees and conflicting branches <a class="header-anchor" href="#dirty-trees-and-conflicting-branches" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dirty trees and conflicting branches&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shared tree is dirty before work</td><td>The delegation refuses: <em>&quot;⚠️ <code>&lt;project&gt;</code> has uncommitted changes. Commit or stash them first, then ask me again.&quot;</em></td></tr><tr><td>Shared tree is dirty, but the dirt is this run&#39;s own branch being resumed</td><td>Allowed. After a crash killed a worker mid-edit, the dirt <strong>is</strong> the in-progress work; the commit phase sweeps it into the resumed change</td></tr><tr><td>Worktree is dirty</td><td>Always allowed — the worktree belongs to this run alone, so the dirt is never another run&#39;s edits</td></tr><tr><td>The run branch is already checked out in another worktree</td><td><code>git worktree add</code> fails; the run falls back to the shared tree under the repo lock</td></tr><tr><td>A remembered branch has been deleted</td><td>The run self-heals onto its stable run-branch name; if that name already exists it is reused, never silently forked</td></tr><tr><td>Work is cancelled mid-edit</td><td>Uncommitted edits are stashed (<code>saidi cancelled: &lt;task&gt;</code>); a freshly-cut branch is deleted only if nothing landed and the stash succeeded</td></tr><tr><td>Switching the run&#39;s branch from the CODE view with a dirty tree</td><td>Refused: <em>&quot;…has uncommitted changes — switching branches now could lose them.&quot;</em></td></tr></tbody></table><p>One subtlety in the shared-tree path: after committing, the tree is restored to base — <strong>unless</strong> base is itself a <code>saidi/run-*</code> branch. That would be a previous run&#39;s leftover rather than a human&#39;s checkout, and switching onto it once yanked the checkout out from under a live sibling agent mid-verification. Staying on this run&#39;s branch is the safe default.</p><p>Because the shared tree is restored to base, anything that inspects the repo afterwards is looking at base, not at the work. That is why mechanical checks without a worktree run inside <code>withBranchCheckedOut</code> under the repo lock — see <a href="/docs/concepts/verification">Verification &amp; Evidence</a>.</p><h2 id="cleanup" tabindex="-1">Cleanup <a class="header-anchor" href="#cleanup" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cleanup&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Worktrees are released when a run ends and when it is deleted.</p><ul><li><code>releaseRunResources</code> calls <code>removeRunWorktrees</code> for runs ending <code>done</code> or <code>cancelled</code>. Worktrees are <strong>kept on <code>error</code></strong> so a failed run&#39;s tree can still be inspected.</li><li>Removal first reaps processes still running inside the directory. A dev server holds its cwd open; removing the directory under it would leave a live process serving files that no longer exist while still owning the port. What was stopped is reported into the run thread — a dev server that vanishes without a trace is indistinguishable from one that crashed.</li><li>Then <code>git worktree remove --force</code>, a direct <code>rm -rf</code> of the directory (some git versions refuse a dirty tree even with <code>--force</code>, and the directory is this run&#39;s alone), and <code>git worktree prune</code>.</li><li>The whole <code>repos/</code> root is removed, which takes the hydrated <code>node_modules</code> with it — roughly 800 MB per project worktree, the bulk of what a finished run occupies on disk.</li><li>If the project left the registry mid-run there is no repo to hand the worktree back to; the directory and anything running in it are still cleaned up.</li></ul><p><code>git worktree prune</code> is what keeps the repo&#39;s administrative state honest after a crash. Without it a killed run leaves a registration behind and the branch it held stays &quot;checked out&quot; forever — which would turn the isolation guarantee into a permanent lockout of that branch.</p><p><strong>The branch always survives.</strong> It is the deliverable, and publishing a PR still needs it. Deleting a run removes Saidi&#39;s record and (by default) its on-disk assets and workspaces; the git branches are untouched.</p><p>See also: <a href="/docs/concepts/runs">Runs</a> · <a href="/docs/concepts/verification">Verification &amp; Evidence</a> · <a href="/docs/reference/project-policy">Project Policy</a> · <a href="/docs/reference/run-tools">Run Tools</a></p>',21))])}const v=s(p,[["render",u]]);export{w as __pageData,v as default};
